{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:e8896f22b5a75522b275c10ba49f7b4950c17f19271ce6a04d91dcc13a2c514b","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.45},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.5},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.75}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"MY EFFORT'S CORE QUESTION. Five seats: 1.60 / 2.00 / 1.95 / 1.80 / 1.15. DECOMPOSITION, G7 GDP-weighted 2032-36 average = r* + real term premium - repression wedge. The decisive observation, which the Bayesian seat made first and I confirm: all five agree on r* within 20bp (0.80-1.00) and on the direction of the wedge, and disagree ONLY on the real term premium (0.55 / 0.75 / 1.10 / 1.15 / 1.30). This is a term-premium question, and that is where my weighting work goes. COMPONENT 1, r* = 0.90%. I adopt FERMI'S component arithmetic as authoritative — it is the best single piece of work in the effort: a 2026 GDP-weighted base of 0.93% (US 1.3 at weight 0.48, euro area 0.5, Japan 0.15, UK 1.0, France 0.5, Italy 0.3, Canada 1.5) and a signed change list netting to ~0bp — pure demography +5 (life-cycle dissaving +25 against the still-dominant longevity saving leg -20), public dissaving and rollover volume +35, AI/grid loanable-funds demand +10 (a $0.7-1.1T/yr claim on ~$25T of world gross saving, haircut by the impairment prior), precautionary saving -20, cognitive-compression income effect -10 (BIS 2026 Box C), Chinese and OECD glut recycling -20. The red-team argued precautionary saving was undersized at -20bp, citing Japan post-1995, Korea post-2015 and Germany post-2003 as -80 to -150bp on a domestic neutral rate; that is HALF a hit — the direction is right but those episodes embed far larger employment-security shocks than a 38%-probable 3.5pp compensation-share move, so I take -30bp rather than -20bp and land r* at 0.90. I DOWN-WEIGHT the Scout's hawkish-Fed signal as evidence for a high r*: 3.75-4.00% nominal against 3.1% core is a 0.7-0.9% real policy rate, which is behind-the-curve, not high-neutral. COMPONENT 2, real term premium = 0.95%. The Bayesian's likelihood ratio is the strongest evidentiary argument in the effort and I credit it: DFII10 2.60% on 30 Oct 2026 (FRED, verifying the locked 2A figure exactly) and 2.62% on 2 Nov with DFII30 3.06-3.08%, i.e. a POSITIVE real 10s30s slope of ~+45bp, alongside a JGB 30y all-time high of 4.235% intraday on 5 Oct 2026 and a 10y JGB peak near 3.12%; three independent sovereigns repricing duration simultaneously cannot be generated by a US-idiosyncratic fiscal story, and Japan's participation specifically rules out the high-marginal-product-of-capital reading of a high real rate. The UK 30y gilt at 1998 highs is UNVERIFIED at the Scout's horizon and carries zero weight; the ACM level is also unverified at that horizon (nearest 0.73% at 30 Apr 2026, ~0.83% at 15 May 2026, CEIC 0.782 for January 2026; the 0.96% dated 18 Sep 2026 is Kim-Wright not ACM; the 1.76% year-end figure is explicitly 'a statistical projection, not an observed estimate'), so I do not lean on it. That evidence earns the LEVEL — the reconstructed G7-weighted 2026 premium is ~0.92% — but it does NOT earn further widening, and the red-team landed THREE real hits that deny it. (i) The aggregate debt-supply elasticity has the wrong time-series sign for reserve-currency sovereigns: US federal debt/GDP went 62% -> 100% over 2007-2012, i.e. +76 to +152bp on the 2-4bp-per-1pp elasticity the Historian and Fermi leaned on, while the US 10y TIPS yield FELL ~310bp from +2.4% to -0.7%; Japan reached ~250% debt/GDP at a ~0% real 10y; 1981-2000 US debt rose and real yields fell ~500bp. Fiscal deterioration and real-yield collapse share a cause. That is a cross-sectional spread story (Italy) mistaken for an aggregate-level one. (ii) The issuance-MIX channel: the first-line response to a rollover-cost problem is not paying the term premium, it is not issuing duration — bill-heavy issuance and WAM management truncate the 10-year point mechanically, with no announcement and no legislation. (iii) The largest structural duration BUYER in history arrives inside the window: OECD pension and insurance balance sheets at peak retiree-liability matching under LDI, Solvency II matching-adjustment and IORP rules — a demographic force Wave 1 counted only on the supply side. I therefore set the premium at 0.95%, essentially FLAT on its reconstructed 2026 base, and I explicitly REFUSE Fermi's +38bp of further widening and his 'essentially no mean reversion' conclusion, and the Scout's shading up from 1.75 to 1.95 on trend-following (extrapolating a regime trend to a 2032-36 average is a structural claim, not telemetry, and is outside his own stated lens). COMPONENT 3, repression wedge = -0.35%. Seats: 0.40 / 0.45 / 0.45 / 0.42 / 0.60 on probability. The red-team's widening of the DEFINITION is a real hit on the Wave-1 framing that the US is 'the least likely adopter': the US ran an explicit 2.5% long-bond ceiling 1942-1951 plus Regulation Q, ran a de facto cap in 2020 without the label, and repression today operates through SLR calibration, bank and money-fund liquidity rules creating captive Treasury demand, Basel endgame softening, buyback and WAM management, and statutory reserve-asset requirements on new instrument classes — several live policy now. But his 0.60 is partly definitional inflation: widen the definition far enough and the base rate approaches 1.0, which makes any specific number arbitrary. I hold the tighter test (>= 50bp of suppression below the free-market level over a sustained stretch of 2032-36, on >= 0.80 of G7 GDP weight) at P = 0.52, conditional wedge ~-70bp, giving ~-29bp, plus Japan's existing ~100bp at 0.10 weight already inside the base: -0.35 expected, bimodal (0 or -75 to -150bp). SUM: 0.90 + 0.95 - 0.35 = 1.50%. SECOND METHOD, the red-team's mixture correction, which is his best structural hit — the panel assigned 66-80% to an impairment event and then built a central case conditional on the bust NOT having happened, parking its own modal world in the P10. I honour it but resize it: AI-Q4 resolution is NOT the same event as a macro bust depressing 2032-36 real rates (its second limb is a revenue-coverage test, and a 2027-32 impairment can be digested before the window opens), so P(macro-relevant bust depressing the window average) = 0.45, not 0.70. E = 0.45(1.00) + 0.55(1.90) = 0.45 + 1.045 = 1.495. The two methods agree at 1.50, which is the strongest internal check in my payload. MEAN-REVERSION ASSUMPTION, stated because the brief says it matters more than the level: the G7 GDP-weighted 10y real starts at ~1.90-2.00% (the Scout's composition arithmetic and Fermi's implied-US back-out agree, ~60bp BELOW DFII10 because Japan sits near 1.1% and euro core 1.2-1.5%), so 1.50 assumes ~-45bp of reversion over six to ten years — not the Wave-1 'no reversion' and not a return to the 2010s. Anchoring on the US TIPS print alone is the modal error in this question and biases it high by ~60bp; note also the Scout's 13-day path into 30 Oct (2.88, 2.84, 2.78, 2.74, 2.80, 2.77, 2.76, 2.64, 2.66, 2.66, 2.64, 2.67, 2.63, 2.60, 2.62) shows a ~26bp rally INTO the print, so the locked anchor is the low of its own fortnight. VARIANCE ATTRIBUTION: real term premium sigma 0.55 (~45% of total variance, and essentially ALL of the variance correlated with the demographics x AI coupling, because the coupling acts through issuance volume, convenience-yield erosion and marginal-buyer fragility — all term-premium objects); repression wedge sigma 0.40 (~24% of variance but FIRST in left-tail SHAPE, being bimodal); r* sigma 0.35 (~18%) and largely orthogonal, since aging moves the life-cycle saving elasticity in both directions at once. P10 = 0.45 (heavy repression plus the bust plus a BIS-style demand deficit plus a flight-to-quality bid; a five-year average near zero real has 2011-13 and 2020-21 precedent). P90 = 2.75 (fiscal dominance expressed in YIELDS not caps, basis-trade unwind, premium past 200bp, no repression response — the Wave-1 central case as my upper decile). RESPECTING THE 2A DISTINCTION: see coupling_tension — my g delivers -0.70pp and my r delivers +0.20pp of the locked +1.0pp (r-g) deterioration, so I reproduce ~0.90pp once rather than booking it twice (Wave 1) or under-delivering it (red-team), with r* itself flat-to-easing exactly as 2A's 'frictionless r* eases' permits.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:58.584Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"The NY Fed ACM or Kim-Wright US 10y real term premium prints BELOW +0.40% as a trailing twelve-month average WHILE the real 10s30s slope (DFII30 minus DFII10) flattens to under +15bp, with no new G7 purchase programme, buyback expansion, SLR recalibration or statutory captive-demand mandate announced in the interim.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the ONE piece of evidence my CQ4 level rests on — the 2.5:1 multi-sovereign duration-repricing signal — without the repression explanation that would otherwise save me. It would mean 2026 was a transient selloff of the 2013-14 type. CQ4 P50 falls ~40bp to ~1.10% and the distribution loses its right skew (P90 to ~2.2%); CQ5 falls from 19 to ~12; CQ6-C falls ~5pp and CQ6-D rises ~4pp; CQ3 essentially unaffected, since my growth answer rests on demographic arithmetic rather than on the yield.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"FRED DFII10 twelve-month trailing average >= 2.75% AND G7 GDP-weighted 10y real yield (nominal benchmark minus 10y inflation swap) >= 2.40% on a trailing-twelve-month basis, with no material repression measure enacted in any G7 sovereign on my tighter definition (>= 50bp of suppression sustained).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies BOTH my ~-45bp mean-reversion assumption and my 0.52 repression probability at once, and vindicates the Fermi/Scout seats I down-weighted. CQ4 P50 rises ~65bp to ~2.15% and P10 to ~1.20%; CQ5 rises from 19 to ~29 via the MOVE conjunct; CQ6-C rises ~6pp and CQ6-A falls ~5pp; CQ2's OECD conjunct falls ~4pp, since the inflation-tolerant wedge I priced would not be operating.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >= 25% occurs AND G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yields rise >= 25bp (or fail to fall 50bp) inside the drawdown window.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my central reconciliation on CQ5's second conjunct — that a capex-bust drawdown is disinflationary and bond-bullish (2000-02, 2007-09, Japan 1989-92), which is where I moved toward the red-team and away from Wave 1's 2022 template. CQ5's conjunct B resolves YES on its own, so CQ5 collapses to conjunct A and rises from 19 to ~33-36; CQ4 P90 fattens toward 3.1 and P50 rises ~25bp; CQ6-C rises ~5pp at D's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Realized PPP-weighted real GDP growth for OECD-38 + China over 2027-2031 (IMF WEO / OECD Economic Outlook, constant-PPP aggregation) comes in at or below 1.55%/yr, with China's own 2029-2031 average below 2.5%.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my partial acceptance of the red-team's double-subtraction correction — it would mean the locked 2A coefficients ARE counterfactual-net and the Wave-1 seats were right to subtract them whole, and that my -0.75pp increment should have been -1.05pp. CQ3 P50 falls from 1.95 to ~1.65 with P90 compressing to ~2.65; CQ6-A collapses from 20 to ~10, CQ6-D rises from 17 to ~24, CQ6-B toward ~50; CQ4 P50 falls ~20bp on the r* and capex-demand legs; hypothesis plank (d) falls from 15 to ~9.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Reported strict-operational data-centre electricity consumption across OECD-38 + China for calendar year 2032 comes in at or below 700 TWh (against ~365 TWh in 2024) WHILE publicly identified cancelled-or-impaired announced capacity exceeds 25% of the 2025-2027 announcement book.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my reconciliation on the coupling tension specifically — I held AI-Q3's P50 roughly unchanged at 930-1,000 TWh on the red-team's fibre-1999-2002 argument that impairment destroys the claim and not the asset, against my own effort's unanimous Wave-1 push to 600-880 TWh. This observation would vindicate them and refute me. My CQ3 TFP and capital-deepening terms drop ~0.15pp (P50 to ~1.80); CQ6-A down ~4pp and CQ6-D up ~4pp; CQ4 P50 down ~15bp as the AI/grid loanable-funds claim vanishes; my modest upward revision of AI-Q1 to 26-30% is withdrawn.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'rstar'."}}